WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.322
For the first version of RAG, we only need a small set of libraries.

00:00:04.322 --> 00:00:07.582
Pandas will manage the document and chunk tables.

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:13.707
NumPy will hold our vectors and help with ranking.

00:00:13.707 --> 00:00:17.047
Sentence Transformers will create embeddings.

00:00:17.047 --> 00:00:23.377
Scikit-learn is useful for similarity calculations, and the OpenAI Python client can connect to a language

00:00:23.377 --> 00:00:28.492
model later. Notice what we are not installing yet.

00:00:28.492 --> 00:00:34.852
We are not starting with several vector databases, LangChain, or a large orchestration framework.

00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:36.017
That is intentional.

00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:43.762
When too many libraries are introduced at the beginning, it becomes difficult to tell which part of the

00:00:43.762 --> 00:00:46.252
system is actually doing the retrieval.

00:00:46.252 --> 00:00:52.652
Start with the simplest environment that can demonstrate the architecture.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:57.787
Once that works, adding a vector database becomes a meaningful engineering choice rather than another

00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:00.012
mysterious package.
